+# Client posture and reconnect on posture change
+
+Moonlight tells a posture-aware host (Vibepollo with the stream-posture-sync
+patches) whether this client is in a touch-primary state, so the host can adapt
+its virtual display, for example to dock the Windows touch keyboard. Hosts that
+do not know the arguments ignore them.
+
+## What is sent
+
+`clientPosture` (`desktop`, `laptop`, `tablet`) and `clientTouch` (`0`/`1`) on
+the launch and resume request, and on `/posture` while streaming. Nothing about
+the screen size or DPI is sent; this client reports every touch-primary state as
+`tablet`. Setting **Touch tablet posture** (`--client-posture auto|tablet|never`)
+overrides detection.
+
+Auto mode on a 2-in-1 reports `tablet` while the OS tablet mode is on (KDE
+`tabletMode`, Windows `SM_CONVERTIBLESLATEMODE` == 0), that is while the keyboard
+is detached or flipped, and `laptop` while it is attached.
+
+## Live sync and reconnect
+
+While streaming, a worker thread watches the posture. On Linux/KDE it
+subscribes to the KWin `org.kde.KWin.TabletModeManager` `tabletModeChanged`
+D-Bus signal and also samples once a second as a fallback; on Windows it
+samples once a second. A change is debounced for 1 second and sent to the host
+as `/posture`.
+
+The host only rebuilds its virtual display on a resume. When the host accepted a
+change that crosses the touch boundary (tablet <-> laptop/desktop), the client
+therefore resumes the same app: it tears the stream down without quitting the
+host app and starts it again with `resume`. This is the same segue that replaces
+the stream view when a game is quit and another started, so it works for GUI and
+`moonlight stream` launches alike; a CLI launch relaunches the stream instead of
+exiting.
+
+Setting **Reconnect when posture changes** (default on, `--reconnect-on-posture`
+/ `--no-reconnect-on-posture`) controls this. Safety rules:
+
+- at most one automatic restart per 60 seconds; a flip blocked by that cooldown
+  is applied when it ends, but only if the posture still differs from the one
+  the running display was created for
+- never while connecting: nothing happens in the first 5 seconds of a stream
+- never loops: the restarted stream starts from the current posture, so that is
+  its baseline
+- skipped when the stream is already ending (quit, signal, connection loss)
+- changes between touch classes, or touch-ness changes alone, never restart
+
+If the resume fails, the usual launch error is shown.
